{
  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about task difficulty, the value of each modality, or the benefit of added complexity (multiprofile, transformer aggregation, fusion). The most critical unknown is the performance ceiling of a simple, well-understood single-modality model. 2) What this tests: config_002 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the cleanest, most standard baseline in cellular profiling—RNA features reduced by PCA to 2000 dimensions with no image, no multiprofile, no aggregation, no fusion. It establishes the reference accuracy/F1 that every multimodal or multiprofile configuration must beat to justify its added complexity and computational cost. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: With a limited budget, the highest-information first move is a minimal, fast, interpretable baseline. It (a) calibrates the difficulty of the classification task, (b) provides the anchor for isolating the marginal contribution of the image modality, multiprofile aggregation, and fusion strategy in all subsequent comparisons, and (c) is computationally cheap, leaving budget for follow-up experiments. Without this reference, any multimodal result would be uninterpretable."
}

